00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:21.000
for example, if it
is a Parquet file--

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:23.890
obviously you'll have
a much larger data set.

00:28:23.890 --> 00:28:26.190
And in a lot of
our customers, they

00:28:26.190 --> 00:28:30.240
want to store the cold data in
a Hadoop system or a data lake

00:28:30.240 --> 00:28:34.430
system, so the profiling task
for a Hadoop system, or even

00:28:34.430 --> 00:28:37.140
for a cloud system, can be
enabled similar to what you're

00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:41.460
seeing here from a HANA table.

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:43.590
Now I enter into the
Pipeline Modeler.

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:48.554
So the idea here is, as a data
engineer or a data modeler,

00:28:48.554 --> 00:28:49.970
or even a data
scientist, you want

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:52.790
to understand the data a little
bit before you can really

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:54.210
play around with the data.

00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:56.230
So if you are a data
scientist, you're

00:28:56.230 --> 00:29:00.800
using the Jupyter Notebooks,
or IPython Notebooks.

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:03.890
In our case, we provide
this Data Hub tool,

00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:08.720
where you have a UI-enabled
way of dragging and dropping

00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:11.919
information and trying
to build your pipelines.

00:29:11.919 --> 00:29:13.460
So here, you're
looking at a pipeline

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:18.020
where we've used the data that
we got from the sales order

00:29:18.020 --> 00:29:23.390
table, and we're running
some predictive algorithms.

00:29:23.390 --> 00:29:26.690
In this case, we're going to
be using Python initially.

00:29:26.690 --> 00:29:29.600
And in the next phase,
we're going to be using R.

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:34.400
So we'll show you how you
can very easily modify

00:29:34.400 --> 00:29:37.450
your predictive algorithms
to suit whatever

00:29:37.450 --> 00:29:38.660
