00:29:38.660 --> 00:29:41.180
So if you are a
TensorFlow customer,

00:29:41.180 --> 00:29:44.270
you can just strip and
replace a Python algorithm

00:29:44.270 --> 00:29:47.210
with a TensorFlow algorithm, and
you can enable the prediction.

00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:49.280
So we have two tasks here.

00:29:49.280 --> 00:29:51.580
One is training the
algorithm, and the other one

00:29:51.580 --> 00:29:53.330
is the serving part.

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:57.110
And now I'm showing you
how we build a training

00:29:57.110 --> 00:29:59.870
algorithm using Python.

00:29:59.870 --> 00:30:02.630
So I'm opening up my
SQL generator, which

00:30:02.630 --> 00:30:05.780
is where I'm getting the
data from my HANA system,

00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:08.130
so the sales order
table that you just saw.

00:30:08.130 --> 00:30:10.010
I'm going to bring
in that data, and I'm

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:13.160
going to convert
that to a CSV format,

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:16.140
so that I can feed that
to my Python model,

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:18.800
so that it can take
that data and then do

00:30:18.800 --> 00:30:21.590
analysis on that Python model.

00:30:21.590 --> 00:30:25.730
So we leverage some of
the open-source libraries,

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:28.640
like Pandas and [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:32.480
So again, I can bring in
any external algorithm.

00:30:32.480 --> 00:30:35.690
I can make that available
as part of my pipeline,

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:39.860
and leverage the insights
that I get out of that.

00:30:39.860 --> 00:30:41.330
So what you're
seeing here is, this

00:30:41.330 --> 00:30:44.240
is called a graph, or a
pipeline, within Data Hub.

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:48.180
And each of those blocks
are the operators.

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:51.800
So we deliver around
200-plus operators

00:30:51.800 --> 00:30:53.510
to do different
tasks, whether it's

00:30:53.510 --> 00:30:55.630
you want to anonymize
the data in-flight,

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:57.710
you want to do
data cleansing, you

00:30:57.710 --> 00:31:00.350
want to do geocoding,
reverse geocoding,

00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:03.470
you want to enable location
intelligence on top of that,

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:06.500
or you, as a partner, want
to build your own operator.

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:08.550
We provide all
those capabilities

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:12.380
as part of Data Hub,
and extend that even

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:15.180
to machine learning
and AI as well.

00:31:15.180 --> 00:31:19.640
So I'm just showing some
open-source libraries

00:31:19.640 --> 00:31:22.210
that I'm importing from Pandas.

00:31:22.210 --> 00:31:25.610
This is the Python
library that we've

00:31:25.610 --> 00:31:28.820
written for doing the
predictive analysis based

00:31:28.820 --> 00:31:33.570
on the sales orders data.

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:36.920
So just switching
through the demo.

00:31:36.920 --> 00:31:39.870
So what I have here is,
when I execute this,

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:41.790
I'm training this model.

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:44.770
So there is first the training
phase, which I'm doing.

00:31:44.770 --> 00:31:46.560
And then, once I've
trained it, I'm

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:49.110
going to serve the
model so that I

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:52.370
can apply this model on
new data that's coming in,

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:54.300
or real-time data
that's coming in,

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:57.220
to be able to get a prediction
out of that real-time data.

00:32:07.850 --> 00:32:10.860
So let me quickly
move through this.

00:32:10.860 --> 00:32:13.480
So basically, what you're
seeing here is right now,

00:32:13.480 --> 00:32:16.740
I have the train model,
or the serving model.

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:20.750
So I've used the Python
serve model here.

00:32:20.750 --> 00:32:23.750
And I'm going to be
loading the data that I

00:32:23.750 --> 00:32:26.660
got from my trained ML model.

00:32:26.660 --> 00:32:29.750
I'm going to feed that through
this prediction Python,

00:32:29.750 --> 00:32:31.850
and then get a prediction.

00:32:31.850 --> 00:32:36.360
So based on the inputs that you
provide for this prediction,

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:39.740
you'll be able to get a
prediction from that model

00:32:39.740 --> 00:32:43.400
saying, depending on the
customer and their buying

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:47.130
pattern, if I provide
number of days,

00:32:47.130 --> 00:32:49.640
there is a high probability
that the customer is going

00:32:49.640 --> 00:32:52.230
to buy another similar product.

00:32:58.160 --> 00:33:02.070
So here I'm picking up
the customer ID, customer

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:03.540
information, and the product.

00:33:03.540 --> 00:33:05.580
And then I specify the day.

00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:07.440
And based on the
number of days, it's

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:10.410
going to tell me, what's the
probability that the customer

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:12.090
might buy a product?

00:33:12.090 --> 00:33:15.510
So this is, again, based on the
historic information about what

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:17.130
this customer has bought.

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:19.660
There's a certain level of
recommendation capabilities

00:33:19.660 --> 00:33:20.160
as well.

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:24.990
So when I punch in 50 days,
there's 1.51 times probability.

00:33:24.990 --> 00:33:27.600
When I punch in 500
days, there's a 14 times

00:33:27.600 --> 00:33:32.710
probability that the customer
might buy this product.

00:33:32.710 --> 00:33:37.810
So now I switch from
using a Python algorithm

00:33:37.810 --> 00:33:39.980
to a R algorithm.

00:33:39.980 --> 00:33:43.330
So we'll show you
how easy it is.

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:45.440
So we've already
created a graph.

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:48.610
So what we do is, first,
we copy this pipeline.

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:49.830
So I already have a pipeline.

00:33:49.830 --> 00:33:52.180
So this is the whole
reusability part

00:33:52.180 --> 00:33:57.820
of how a developer can reuse
pipelines that are already

00:33:57.820 --> 00:34:00.662
existing in your repository.

00:34:00.662 --> 00:34:02.870
So I'm just going to call
this with a different name.

00:34:02.870 --> 00:34:07.420
I'm going to call it Train
for R. And then I go in.

00:34:07.420 --> 00:34:08.690
I remove this.

00:34:08.690 --> 00:34:12.510
So I'm just literally going
to delete that operator.

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:15.969
And I'm going to drag
and drop a R operator.

00:34:15.969 --> 00:34:17.969
So obviously, depending on--

00:34:17.969 --> 00:34:20.139
and like you see there
on the left side,

00:34:20.139 --> 00:34:23.719
it could be a TensorFlow
operator as well.

00:34:23.719 --> 00:34:27.570
So here I'm looking at the
code from a R standpoint.

00:34:27.570 --> 00:34:30.400
So this is a custom code
that I've written for R.

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:34.870
And then I just have to connect
my operator to the input data

00:34:34.870 --> 00:34:37.510
feed that I'm
getting, and then I'll

00:34:37.510 --> 00:34:42.270
be ready to train this
graph using the new R

00:34:42.270 --> 00:34:47.384
operator, R code, that I just
inserted into this pipeline.

00:34:49.866 --> 00:34:51.699
So I just have to make
some changes in terms

00:34:51.699 --> 00:34:54.610
of what the model name is.

00:34:54.610 --> 00:34:57.460
I'll go through that.

00:34:57.460 --> 00:35:01.550
It's a similar exercise as
we did in the previous time.

00:35:01.550 --> 00:35:03.860
Now I'm running the train model.

00:35:03.860 --> 00:35:05.830
Once I complete this,
